STDLIB(3)                      P32W Unix                       STDLIB(3)

NAME
       stdlib, abs, labs, rand, srand - absolute value, random numbers

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       int abs(int v);
       long labs(long v);
       int rand(void);
       void srand(unsigned seed);

DESCRIPTION
       stdlib.h declares the general utilities of the library,
       documented on these pages:

       malloc(3)    malloc, free, calloc, realloc
       atoi(3)      atoi, atol, strtol, strtoul
       qsort(3)     qsort, bsearch
       getenv(3)    getenv, setenv, unsetenv, environ
       exit(3)      exit, atexit, abort; _exit is exit(2)
       stdlib(3)    abs, labs, rand, srand

       It also defines NULL and size_t, EXIT_SUCCESS (0) and
       EXIT_FAILURE (1), and RAND_MAX (32767).

       abs and labs return the absolute value of v (int and long
       are the same 32 bits).

       rand returns the next number of a pseudo-random sequence, 0 to
       RAND_MAX. It is the generator of the C standard's example:
       next = next * 1103515245 + 12345, returning bits 16 to 30 of
       next. srand starts the sequence again from seed; a program
       that does not call srand gets the sequence of seed 1.

EXAMPLES
       srand(time(0) ^ getpid());
       die = rand() % 6 + 1;

SEE ALSO
       malloc(3), atoi(3), qsort(3), getenv(3), exit(3), time(2)

BUGS
       abs(INT_MIN) is INT_MIN. rand is weak: its low bits repeat
       with short periods and its sequence repeats after 2^32 calls;
       it is not for cryptography. There is no div, ldiv, random,
       atof, strtod, mkstemp or system.
